.Dd July 30, 2026
.Dt CLOVE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clove
.Nd control an I2P-only BitTorrent daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -socket Ar path
.Ar command
.Op Ar arguments
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the control program for
.Xr cloved 8 ,
an I2P-only BitTorrent daemon.
It sends one request per invocation over the daemon's local HTTP API
.Pq a unix socket by default
and prints the reply as an aligned table, or verbatim JSON with
.Fl -json .
.Pp
The socket path and the API token are read from the same configuration the
daemon uses, so
.Nm
normally needs no arguments beyond a command.
The token is read from
.Pa <data_dir>/token ,
which the daemon creates mode 0600 on first start; running
.Nm
therefore requires the same user as the daemon, or read access to that file.
.Pp
A command names a torrent by its info-hash, by any prefix of that hash long
enough to be unique among the hosted torrents — four characters at the least —
or by its position in
.Cm list ,
the number in the
.Sy #
column.
A position is one to three digits, which is why it can never be mistaken for a
hash prefix: the shortest prefix is four characters.
A position means whatever the listing says at that moment and is resolved
against a listing fetched for the command, so it is a convenience for typing,
never something to store in a script.
A prefix that matches more than one torrent is an error listing the ones it
matched; it is never resolved by choosing.
A full 40-character hash is matched exactly and is never treated as a prefix,
so a script that names torrents in full is unaffected by what else gets added.
.Pp
The commands marked
.Ar torrent ...
take any number of torrents, or
.Fl -all
for every hosted one.
Each is acted on separately, and one failure does not abandon the rest: the
failures are reported per torrent and the command exits 1 if any of them
failed.
The exception is a daemon that cannot be reached, which stops the command
where it stands.
.Pp
Text that came out of a torrent
.Pq its name, its file paths, its tracker URLs
is chosen by whoever made that torrent, so
.Nm
does not print it to a terminal unaltered: control characters and the
bidirectional overrides are replaced with
.Ql \&. ,
and a name too long for its column is elided with
.Ql … .
Without this a torrent could name itself in escape sequences and repaint the
screen of anyone who ran
.Cm list .
.Fl -json
is unaffected and always carries the torrent's true name — the daemon's JSON
escapes control characters as
.Ql \eu00XX
rather than removing them.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -socket Ar path
Talk to the daemon listening on
.Ar path
instead of the configured socket.
.It Fl -json
Print the API's JSON reply unchanged.
Accepted by every read-only command
.Pq Cm status , Cm list , Cm show .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a command summary and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm status Op Fl -json
Everything about the client at once, in three blocks.
What is answering: version, router connection state, SAM address, and uptime.
What it is doing: the torrent count broken down by state, the client-wide
transfer rates, and the peers attached across every torrent against the
.Ic peer_limit
they come from.
What it has moved: the bytes downloaded and uploaded over the lifetime of
every hosted torrent.
.Pp
The router state is one of
.Sy connecting ,
.Sy waiting-for-router
or
.Sy connected .
.Pp
.Fl -json
is the reply from
.Ic GET /v1/status
unchanged.
The state breakdown and the lifetime totals are not in it: they are summed
here from the torrent listing for a human to read, and a second JSON shape
that only this program emits would be a schema nothing versions.
Ask for
.Cm list Fl -json
and add them up, or see
.Xr clove-api 7 .
.It Cm list Op Fl -json
A summary bar, then one line per torrent: its position, progress, state, size,
current down
.Pq Sy ▼
and up
.Pq Sy ▲
rates, connected peers against known peers, and its name.
.Pp
The bar above the table is the same reading
.Cm status
opens with — version, router, uptime, torrent count, client-wide rates and
peers — so a listing answers "and how is the client doing" without a second
command.
.Pp
Progress is a bar and a figure in one column, and both round down: a torrent
shows
.Ql 100%
and a full bar only once it holds every piece it asked for.
Columns are a fixed width rather than measured from the rows, so a listing
being repainted
.Pq Ic watch -n 2 clove list
does not shuffle its columns sideways when a rate gains a digit.
.Pp
Torrents are listed in the order they were added, which does not change as
others are added or removed — so a row does not move out from under you while
you are reading it.
A rate of
.Ql -
means nothing is moving, printed that way rather than as a zero so the torrents
that are doing something stand out.
.Pp
The info-hash is not a column.
A torrent is named to other commands by its
.Sy #
here, or by a prefix from
.Cm show ;
printing forty characters no eye reads on every row cost more width than it
returned.
.It Cm show Ar torrent Op Fl -json
One torrent in detail: sizes, progress, state, peer counts, bytes moved, its
share ratio and the limit it is held to, the file table with per-file
priorities, and the announce URLs.
.Pp
.Sy paused_because
appears whenever the torrent is stopped, and says whether that was the
operator, its seed ratio or the idle limit — so a torrent that stopped on its
own is never just "paused".
.Pp
The peer counts answer different questions.
.Sy peers
is how many are connected right now;
.Sy known_peers
is how many destinations are known and could be dialed;
.Sy pex_peers
is how many of those were first learned from another peer's
.Sy i2p_pex
message rather than from a tracker; and
.Sy inbound_peers
is how many peers dialed
.Em us .
The last two count since the torrent started rather than describing the
present, so a peer that has since disconnected still shows in them.
.Pp
.Sy announces_ok ,
.Sy announces_failed
and
.Sy last_announce_error
answer the first question a torrent with no peers raises: whether its trackers
are replying at all.
.Sy last_announce_error
appears only when an announce has failed, and carries the tracker's own words.
.It Cm add Ar file | Ar magnet Op Fl -paused Op Fl -sequential
Add a torrent from a
.Pa .torrent
file or a
.Ar magnet:\&
URI.
.Pp
.Fl -paused
adds it stopped, and
.Fl -sequential
picks pieces in file order from the outset — both settings a torrent would
otherwise need a second command to change, after it had already started doing
the other thing.
A magnet is stored immediately and listed as
.Sy fetching-metadata
while the daemon fetches its info dictionary from peers; it becomes an
ordinary torrent once that succeeds.
.It Cm remove Ar torrent ... | Fl -all Op Fl -data
Remove torrents.
With
.Fl -data
their downloaded files are deleted as well.
.Pp
.Fl -all Fl -data
deletes the data of every hosted torrent and cannot be undone.
Both flags are required to spell it, which is the only guard there is: a
control program that stopped to ask would be one that cannot be scripted.
.It Cm pause Ar torrent ... | Fl -all
Stop peer acquisition and disconnect these torrents' peers.
The paused state persists across daemon restarts.
.It Cm resume Ar torrent ... | Fl -all
Bring paused torrents back online.
A torrent returns to its own place in the queue, which is the order torrents
were added; if the active limit is already met it waits there as
.Sy queued .
.It Cm start Ar torrent ... | Fl -all
Resume a torrent and put it at the head of the queue, ahead of everything
waiting.
.Pp
For when the queue order is not the order you want right now.
Only one torrent is at the head at a time — a second
.Cm start
means that one instead, not both — and the position is not remembered across a
restart of
.Xr cloved 8 ,
because forcing is a statement about the moment rather than a property of the
torrent.
.It Cm verify Ar torrent ... | Fl -all
Re-read each torrent's data from disk and re-check every piece against the
metainfo hashes.
A torrent must be paused first, since a running one is writing to the same
files.
.It Cm priorities Ar torrent Ar spec
Set per-file priorities from a comma-separated list, one value per file in
the torrent's own order:
.Sy 0
skip,
.Sy 1
normal,
.Sy 2
high.
A running torrent changes course immediately; blocks already requested are
not cancelled.
.Pp
Files do not begin and end on piece boundaries, so a skipped file still
receives whatever shares a piece with a neighbour it is keeping: at most
one piece at each end of it.
A torrent counts as finished, and starts seeding, once every file it was
told to fetch is present; the skipped ones are not waited for.
.It Cm sequential Ar torrent Cm on | Cm off
Pick pieces in file order rather than rarest-first.
Useful for media you want to start watching before the download finishes; it
is worse for the swarm and slightly worse for you, because the rare pieces
you would otherwise have fetched early may leave before you ask for them.
The change takes effect immediately and survives a restart.
Blocks already requested are left alone, so the switch costs nothing already
in flight.
.It Cm seed-ratio Ar torrent Ar ratio
Stop seeding this torrent once it has uploaded
.Ar ratio
times what it downloaded, overriding
.Ic seed_ratio
in
.Xr clove.conf 5 .
.Ar 0
follows the configured value again.
.Pp
Raising it on a torrent that has already stopped for its ratio starts it back
up, so the effect of the change is visible rather than waiting for a restart.
.It Cm completions Ar bash | Ar zsh | Ar fish
Print a shell completion script on standard output.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa ~/.config/clove/clove.conf
Configuration read by both programs; see
.Xr clove.conf 5 .
.It Pa ~/.local/share/clove/token
API token, mode 0600.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clove/clove.sock
Default control socket.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The command succeeded.
.It 1
The daemon was reached but refused or failed the operation.
.It 2
The command line was wrong.
.It 3
The daemon could not be reached.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Add a torrent, then look at what is happening:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove add ~/Downloads/release.torrent
added 58e2fc46a8dc57c78191f079648750b0644d03a2
$ clove list
clove 0.0.1  connected  3h12m  2 torrents  ▼ 82 KiB/s  ▲ 9 KiB/s  11 (200 max.)

  #  PROGRESS         STATE         SIZE          ▼          ▲  PEERS  NAME
  1  ████▏░░░░░  42%  downloading  1 GiB   82 KiB/s          -   4/20  release.iso
  2  ██████████ 100%  seeding      700 MiB        -    9 KiB/s  11/34  notes.tar
.Ed
.Pp
For a live view, repeat it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ watch -n 2 clove list
.Ed
.Pp
The columns do not move as the numbers change, which is what makes that
readable.
There is no full-screen mode and nothing enters raw mode, so there is nothing
to restore however the loop is interrupted.
.Pp
Look at one torrent in detail, by position or by a prefix of its hash:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove show 1
$ clove show 58e2
.Ed
.Pp
Should a prefix be ambiguous, the daemon says which torrents it hit rather
than picking one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove pause 58
clove: that prefix matches 2 torrents; use more characters
.Ed
.Pp
Download only the second of three files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove priorities 58e2 0,1,0
.Ed
.Pp
Start watching a film before it has finished downloading:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove sequential 58e2 on
58e2 now picks pieces in order
.Ed
.Pp
Act on several torrents at once, or on all of them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove pause 58e2 9b1c
paused 58e2
paused 9b1c
$ clove resume --all
.Ed
.Pp
Re-check a torrent's data on disk:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove pause 58e2
$ clove verify 58e2
verified 5721 piece(s) for 58e2
$ clove resume 58e2
.Ed
.Pp
Two operations have no command of their own and are reached through the local
API: forcing a re-announce, and handing a running torrent a peer to dial.
Both are diagnostics rather than daily use — announces happen on the trackers'
own intervals, and peers arrive from trackers, inbound connections and I2P PEX
— so they are spelled out rather than wrapped.
See
.Xr clove-api 7 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl --unix-socket ~/.local/share/clove/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $(cat ~/.local/share/clove/token)" \e
    -X POST http://clove/v1/torrents/58e2.../announce
.Ed
.Pp
The second connects two instances directly, without a tracker.
The first host's destination is the one
.Xr cloved 8
writes to
.Pa destination
in its data directory.
It is deliberately not printed to the log and not served by
.Cm status ,
so that neither reading a torrent listing nor reading a journal ever hands
out an identity.
On the second host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl --unix-socket ~/.local/share/clove/clove.sock \e
    -H "X-Clove-Token: $(cat ~/.local/share/clove/token)" \e
    -X POST --data asubu6d7wsbj7x7b7agamhimpmnlr3ezb7qu5zh2jllyalblxdpq.b32.i2p \e
    http://clove/v1/torrents/58e2.../peers
.Ed
.Pp
Script against the API by asking for JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove list --json | jq -r '.[] | select(.state=="seeding") | .name'
.Ed
.Pp
Install shell completion:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ clove completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/clove
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clove.conf 5 ,
.Xr clove-api 7 ,
.Xr cloved 8
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
speaks only to a local daemon.
It never touches the network itself, and it has no clearnet code path at all:
every byte clove moves goes through I2P.
